A||||5||isthiswhereiputausername?||replacing front inner tierod||||1||
Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-25-2001||06:29 PM||||How hard are they to take off the rack?  I have another rack I was going to replace it with, but it appears like its only my passengers side inner tierod that needs to be changed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Eriamjh||11-25-2001||08:21 PM||||My '87 had a bad inner tie rod and I decided with 155K on it, that I might as well just replace the rack and both outer rod ends.<P>Both my upper bushings on both sides were shot.  The car is getting a complete front end now.<P>If you pull the rack, hit the bolts with bunches of penetrant and prepare for the to either strip (if you don't use a 6-point socket) or just plain break.  I broke 3 of four to remove mine.  Got 2 drilled out with one more to go.  The car has been on jacks for 8 days now and progress is going well.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||11-25-2001||08:29 PM||||I have found it to be much easier to remove the rack if you first remove the tub that holds the spare tire first.  Disconnect the brake lines from the underside of the tub before you try to remove the tub.<P>If the rack is original, be sure to soak the rack mounting bolts for awhile.  I have broken off several due to bad corrosion.  If you do break one or more, use a dremel to grind out as much of the nut as possible and use a chisel to break it free of the frame.  You can then use a regular (high strength) bolt and nut when reassembling.<P>Also remember that you need to completely remove the bolt that secures the steering shaft to the rack, and probably will need to use a screwdriver to spread the shaft to rack connector before it will separate.<P>I just replaced the rack in my blue GT, it took me a couple of hours to disconnect and reconnect everything since I now know what I am doing.  Take your time and it should go OK.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||11-25-2001||08:37 PM||||To remove an inner tie rod end, first remove the tire on that side. then loosen the 22mm or 7/8 nut from between inner and outer tie rod end. now spin inner tie rod end out of the outer tie rod end, counting the turns to aid in alignment. Now remove accordian style boot. but since you are doing the right you must remove the steering shock (easy, no big deal). with boot off and holding steering rack from turning spin off outer tie rod end. apply thread lock to new one and spin on and again tighten holding steering rack form spinning and crimp end to keep from comming off.  Crimp part is easier to see than explain.   This is a simple job with a inner tie rod end tool.  You will need a toe set (alignment) afterwards.<P>Pete<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||jelly2m8||11-25-2001||08:39 PM|||| Your in luck isthiswhereiputausername.<P> The Fiero inner tie rods can be changed on the car, with basic hand tools, without removing the steering rack.<P> Basically you want to remove the outer tierod end, lock nut, the bellows boot, and use a wrench, or cresent wrench to take the inner tierod off.<P> Make note of how many turns the outer tierod end is on the inner, or you can measure the distance from the lock nut to the edge of the treads on the inner tierod.<P> That way you will get the alignment close enough to get to an alignment shop. <P> loosen the lock nut before you do anything else. you can even leave the outer tierod attached to the steering knuckle if you want, by turning the inner tierod out of it.<P> Check the rack bushing on the passanger side, that usually wears out, leaving a suspected bad inner tierod. <P> You can do this by pulling the bellows boot back, and turning the wheels to the right. Take ahold of the end of the steering rack itself ( thats the shaft comming out of the housing) and shaking it vigorously up and down, front and back. There should be no noticable movement if the rack bushing is good.<P> You can use a plastic zip tie on the bellows boot around the rack housing if the inner tierod doesn't come with a new clamp.<P> Always have your car aligned after replacing and steering / suspension components.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-25-2001||08:47 PM||||Thanks for all the info guys!  I will get this done hopefully this week and then she should be ready for her allignment  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
